= Energy efficiency =

== Overview ==
'''Energy efficiency''' describes how much useful service or output is obtained relative to the energy required to provide it.

== Definition ==
Efficiency improves when the same service is delivered with less energy input, or when more service is produced from the same energy input.

== Scope & importance ==
Efficiency can reduce fuel use, operating cost, waste heat, infrastructure load, and environmental impacts without necessarily reducing the service being provided.

== Core concepts ==
Efficiency is usually a ratio of useful output to input, but the exact metric depends on the system. Lighting may use luminous efficacy; motors, buildings, industrial processes, and vehicles use different performance measures.

== Examples ==
An efficient light source produces the required illumination using less electrical energy. Building insulation can reduce the heating or cooling energy needed to maintain indoor conditions.

== Applications / implications ==
Energy efficiency is applied in buildings, industry, transportation, appliances, data centers, electrical systems, and energy policy.

== Epistemic notes ==
Efficiency is not identical to energy conservation or reduced total consumption. System boundaries, rebound effects, output quality, and the energy accounting method can change the interpretation.

